This is something I just noticed for the first time in Nethack, and it's not
only annoying but can even be dangerous as I incidentally noticed...

Typically I have pickup_types set to $?!="/ and also autopickup exceptions
defined, specifically for daggers. In my just finished game I was close to
stressed and wanted to avoid automatically picking up those defined object
classes, but I still wanted the thrown +7 daggers to get picked up by the
exceptions rule to not lose turns on Astral. So I removed the types $?!="/
from the list and kept the @-toggle pickup active.

The situation was as follows; I threw daggers and killed some monster and a
Rider:  @%%   I had to pass those two corpses to get to the next altar. The
first step made me auto-pickup the first corpse! (And if I had moved another
step I would have revived the Rider (by "trying" to pick the corpse up as
well, where I just intended to pass the square). But % was not in the list.

What's actually implemented is that an empty pickup-types list means "pick
up *all* items". (This may probably be what's described as "default" in the
Guidebook[*]?)

I imagine what would have happened if I'd had stepped on the field with the
Rider corpse, and a heap of loot of other monsters that may have been there.
The empty pickup_types list would have made me pick up anything on that spot,
thus making me slower by the load, *and* revive the Rider, who will then get
a couple of turns (because of the load, and the need for a turn to drop it).

I don't know what others think, but I think it's a misfeature as implemented.
In the (unlikely or rare) [special] case that I want to pick up all items -
usually you don't want to do that! - I may explicitly define those items;
there's no need for a special short form, the empty list. The empty list
has it's crucial use in conjunction with auto-pickup exceptions, as shown
in the use-case above.

Janis

[*] Nethack Guidebook:
      pickup_types
        Specify the object types to be picked up when autopickup is on.
        Default is all types.

